# The worker: ingest, the lake write API, the raw loader, the extract verb and the dbt
# transform.
#
# Bun runs TypeScript directly, so there is no build step for our code. dbt is installed as
# a BINARY we invoke -- we author no Python and import nothing from it. It lives here
# rather than in a container the worker starts, because starting a container needs the
# Docker socket and nothing in this stack gets one. See ADR 0007.
FROM oven/bun:1.4.2-debian

# dbt-core and the Postgres adapter, pinned. A venv keeps them off the system Python.
#
# `poppler-utils` is here for the SAME REASON and in the same category: it supplies
# `pdftotext`, which `services/extract/extractText.ts` invokes by name and never imports.
# Without it every PDF in the lake is recorded `extractor-missing:pdftotext` -- the
# extractor refuses by name rather than crashing, so the run stays green and the symptom is
# a Lake division reading "122 documents, 0 readable" with nothing failing anywhere.
# It also supplies `pdftoppm`, which `services/extract/pdfPages.ts` uses to turn a scanned
# page into an image -- so the scanned-PDF reader below costs no new package at all.
#
# `tesseract-ocr` was deferred on the condition this file stated in as many words -- shipping
# a binary nothing calls is weight in the image and an attack surface in the process, so "it
# arrives with the reader that needs it". THE CONDITION IS NOW MET: `services/extract/ocr.ts`
# invokes `tesseract` for `image/png` and `image/jpeg`, which is 1,557 files in the lake that
# no reader could open before. The "200 MB" in that note was an estimate; measured on amd64 on
# top of the three packages above, `tesseract-ocr` is 24 packages and 75.5 MB.
#
# `tesseract-ocr-vie` IS NOT OPTIONAL, and it is 547 kB of that. Of the 409 documents read on
# production, 69 (17%) carry Vietnamese diacritics, and tesseract without the pack does not
# fail on them -- it returns confident English-looking words that were never on the page, which
# reaches a search index looking exactly like data. `which tesseract` answers "installed" and
# cannot answer "can read Vietnamese"; this line is the only place that question is settled.
# Trimming it to save half a megabyte quietly turns a sixth of the corpus into fiction.
#
# `pdf_ocr` is the second caller, and it is why the two packages sit beside each other rather
# than in one line: tesseract's input is an IMAGE, so `tesseract scan.pdf` answers "Pdf reading
# is not supported" and exits 1. A scan is `pdftoppm` first and `tesseract` after, at 150 DPI
# -- measured on a rendered Vietnamese invoice, where 150 kept all 38 accented letters and read
# the page exactly, and 300 and 400 DPI each scored slightly worse for 1.6x and 2.6x the bytes.
# Neither program may be trimmed without the other losing its purpose.
#
# A spreadsheet or a Word document needs no binary at all -- `services/extract/xlsx.ts` and
# `docx.ts` say why.
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends python3 python3-venv poppler-utils \
        tesseract-ocr tesseract-ocr-vie \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
    && python3 -m venv /opt/dbt \
    && /opt/dbt/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir "dbt-core==1.9.1" "dbt-postgres==1.9.0"
ENV PATH="/opt/dbt/bin:${PATH}"

WORKDIR /app

# Install dependencies against the lockfile first, so a source change does not re-resolve.
COPY package.json bun.lock ./
COPY packages ./packages
COPY apps ./apps
RUN bun install --frozen-lockfile --production

# Connector specs are read at run time. There is no dbt project in the image: a tenant's
# project is generated from its models in Postgres right before each build, into a
# directory that is removed after. See packages/db/src/services/dbtProject.ts.
COPY specs ./specs

ENV UNDERCROFT_SPECS_DIR=/app/specs/connectors
EXPOSE 8081

# Idle until called. Kestra invokes the HTTP verbs on a schedule; the worker starts no work
# on its own, so a restart never re-runs a sync.
#
# `--smol` -- "use less memory, but run garbage collection more often" -- because THIS process
# cannot see the budget it is held to. `deploy/compose/docker-compose.server.yml` gives the
# worker a 3 GiB cgroup limit, and the host it shares has 23 GB; JSC sizes its heap against
# what it can see, so it feels no pressure while the kernel is about to SIGKILL it. The limit
# was 1 GiB when this flag was added and `--smol` alone did not hold it -- the number moved in
# the same investigation, and the flag stayed because collecting more often is still right for
# a process paced at three requests a second.
#
# The path that makes this bite is Gmail attachments, and it bites because of how Gmail ships
# bytes: an attachment arrives base64-encoded INSIDE a JSON body, so a 10 MB file is ~13 MB of
# base64 text, ~26 MB once held as UTF-16, plus the parsed object graph holding that string
# again, before `decodeBase64Url` produces the 10 MB we wanted. Each one is a transient several
# times the size of the file, and they are collectable -- the run dies holding garbage, not
# data. Nothing here is buffered: on 2026-09-22 the worker was killed at message 3,199 of 7,786
# with `records_read` still advancing, which is what a transient problem looks like and is not
# what the streaming rewrite in ADR 0033 was for.
#
# It is a GC policy, not a fix for a leak. If a run still reaches the limit with this set, the
# next question is the attachment path itself, not another flag.
CMD ["bun", "--smol", "run", "apps/worker/src/server.ts"]
